## A menu on a right click

`context-menu` opens a menu where the pointer is, on a right click on what it wraps — or the keyboard's menu key, or Shift+F10, under what has the focus. It is a `button-menu` opened otherwise: the same items (`[{ label, icon, href, method, token, confirm, target, checked, items, separator, disabled }]`), the same panel, the same events (`select`, `toggle`), the same agent actions. What it wraps is drawn as it is (default slot); the menu takes no room.

Its items may depend on what was clicked — a row, a node of a stage —: `target`, `{ target, event }`, is said before it opens, and the items the page gives in answer, in the same tick, are the ones shown; none, and nothing opens. A right click with Shift held keeps the browser's own menu.

{% raw %}```twig
<context-menu :items="items" label="Actions on the file" @target="onTarget" @select="onSelect">
    <ul class="files">
        <li v-for="file in files" :data-file="file.id" tabindex="0">[[ file.name ]]</li>
    </ul>
</context-menu>
```{% endraw %}

```js
onTarget({ target }) {
  this.current = target?.closest('[data-file]')?.dataset.file ?? null;
}
```

Opened with the pointer, the menu leaves the focus where it was; from the keyboard, its first item takes it, and Escape gives it back. Scrolling the page under it, resizing or leaving the window closes it.
